 ## Overview
-SparSplat is a generalizable, feed-forward model that maps a set of sparse input images (typically 3) and their camera poses to a pixel-aligned map of 2D Gaussian Splatting (2DGS) parameters in a single forward pass [S1, unofficial]. It solves the joint problem of fast multi-view 3D shape reconstruction and novel view synthesis (NVS) from sparse views, leveraging deep features from foundation models like DINOv2 and MASt3R [S1, unofficial]. It differs from its sibling [MVSGaussian](8985-gsgen.md) by using 2DGS primitives instead of 3D Gaussians, which ensures consistent depth across views and enables coherent TSDF mesh extraction, whereas prior generalizable GS methods are primarily optimized for NVS and fail to produce coherent surface reconstructions [S1, unofficial].
+SparSplat is a generalizable, feed-forward model that maps a set of sparse input images to planar surface elements (2D Gaussian Splatting parameters) in a single forward pass to perform 3D shape reconstruction and novel view synthesis [S1, unofficial]. It differs from its sibling [AnySplat Pipeline](12847-anysplat-pipeline.md) and [FineSplat](15257-finesplat.md) by explicitly optimizing for surface consistency via 2D primitives rather than 3D volume primitives, enabling coherent TSDF mesh extraction [S1, unofficial]. Unlike prior generalizable Gaussian methods [48] which are novel-view synthesis (NVS) purposed and fail to produce coherent surfaces upon TSDF fusion, SparSplat is fit for feed-forward surface reconstruction while maintaining superior NVS capabilities [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** A set of $N$ color images $\{I_i\}_{i=1}^N$ (where $N=3$ during training) with resolution $H \times W$ (typically $640 \times 512$) and their corresponding camera poses $P_i$ (rotation and translation).
-**Outputs:** 
-1. A 2DGS parameter map $O_t = \{s, q, p, \alpha, c\} \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times W \times (2+4+3+1+3)}$ for a target view $P_t$, where $s$ is scale (2D), $q$ is rotation (quaternion, 4D), $p$ is centroid (3D), $\alpha$ is opacity (1D), and $c$ is color (3D).
-2. An explicit 3D triangle mesh $S$ extracted from the splatted depths.
+**Inputs:** A set of $N$ color images $\{I_i\}_{i=1}^N$ where $I_i \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times W \times 3}$ and their corresponding camera poses $\{P_i\}_{i=1}^N$ (rotation and translation). A target view pose $P_t$ is specified for prediction.
+**Outputs:** A pixel-aligned 2D Gaussian Splatting parameter map $O_t = \{s, q, p, \alpha, c\} \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times W \times (2+4+3+1+3)}$ for the target view, where $s$ is scale, $q$ is rotation quaternion, $p$ is centroid, $\alpha$ is opacity, and $c$ is color [S1, unofficial]. Optionally, an explicit 3D triangle mesh $S$ is extracted via TSDF fusion of splatted depths [S1, unofficial].
 
 **Data Structures:**
-The model predicts a dense tensor $O_t$ of shape $H \times W \times 13$. The channels are ordered as:
-- Channels 0-1: Scale $s$ (2 floats)
-- Channels 2-5: Rotation $q$ (4 floats, quaternion)
-- Channels 6-8: Position $p$ (3 floats, derived from depth unprojection)
-- Channel 9: Opacity $\alpha$ (1 float)
-- Channels 10-12: Color $c$ (3 floats)
+The model $\Phi$ predicts the attribute map $O_t$. The layout of $O_t$ at each pixel $(u,v)$ is:
+- $s \in \mathbb{R}^2$: 2D scale parameters.
+- $q \in \mathbb{R}^4$: Rotation quaternion.
+- $p \in \mathbb{R}^3$: 3D centroid position.
+- $\alpha \in \mathbb{R}$: Opacity.
+- $c \in \mathbb{R}^3$: Color (RGB).
 
-**Algorithm Steps:**
+**Algorithm:**
